WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.939 align:start position:0%
 
I'm joined today by Amy Julia Harris  who's an investigative reporter for  reveal from the Center for Investigative  Reporting for the past year she and her  colleagues Shoshana Walter have been  investigating abusive drug rehab  programs around the country including  some that forced the participants to  work for free so Amy it's great to talk  to you you know I've been covering the  sort of scamming nature of some rehab  programs in the sort of for-profit world  and there's of course nonprofit and  for-profit drug rehab programs but you  focus specifically on this issue of  participants effectively working unpaid  at some of the the programs in which  they're supposed to be receiving  treatment first question is how did this  even get on your radar that this was  going on yeah we started looking into  this when we were put on the state court  speed and sort of told to find stories  in court systems anywhere across the  country and we got really interested in  diversion programs and drug courts and  it started with a pretty simple question  where were these diversion programs  sending defendants for drug and alcohol  treatment and what we ended up finding  was that a lot of these drug rehab  programs were a little more that worked  camps for private industry and drug  courts and other people in the criminal  justice system were sending defendants  there because these programs marketed  themselves as free so a lot of  defendants who are in the court system  did not have a lot of money did not have  insurance license to rehab programs had  incredibly long wait lists so courts  said here's this great alternative to  keep people out of prison but a lot of  these nonprofit rehab programs were  exploiting people is what we found out  is in exchange for a sort of free drug  treatment people were supposed to work  at for-profit industries and then turn  over their paychecks to the drug rehab  center and that was supposed to pay for  their treatment but we what we found out  at a lot of these centers was there was  no sort of recover  or addiction classes or treatment it was  just non-stop work sometimes 12 16-hour  days for days on end and people were  just working for free and a lot of  people describe it to us it was sort of  like a slave camp and modern-day  indentured servitude okay so let's get  some of the sort of basics down is this  primarily happening at the suppose you  know not legally net designated as  nonprofit rehab centers is it happening  at a mix of for-profit and nonprofit  centers we found for the most part it  was nonprofit rehab centers many of them  were religious we found one in Oklahoma  that was called Christian alcoholics and  addicts in recovery and it prided itself  on not taking any state or federal  funding and the idea was sort of that  participants the program would pick  themselves up by their bootstraps and  work to pay for their stay so it's a  non-profit rehab program and they were  turning over all of their paychecks to  the nonprofit so these these rehab  centers we found out sort of operate  almost like temp agencies that they have  a contract with a for-profit company and  then all the participants in the drug  rehab program go and staff the companies  we found that people were working and  chicken processing plants manufacturing  plants doing really really often  dangerous and very physical jobs and  then they would just turn over their  paychecks to the nonprofit rehab center  I I hate to necessarily make this  comparison but it's fresh in my mind  because I recently read Jeff Quinn's  amazing book about Jonestown and Jim  Jones so much of what you uncovered  mirrors the way that the Jonestown  Peoples Temple operated which was they  brought people in who would turn over  their paychecks some of them would get  supposed addiction treatment from the  sort of pseudo Christian perspective  that the Jones temple subscribed to they  would also sometimes operate nursing  homes because of the way in which they  operated  sort of being slick with licensing and  and how that all had to happen they  operated at very low cost not actually  paying anybody and taking in people's  Social Security money and other sources  of revenue I mean so much of what you  described rings to me so similar to what  was going on with in the noir in  Northern California that Jim Jones was  doing and I'm guessing that part of the  way that it works Amy also skirts  regulation and labor laws as well yeah I  think I think that's an apt comparison a  lot of people that we talk to you  described some of these programs it's  very cult-like we just published a story  in North Carolina about a program called  recovery connections community which is  a two-year long residential rehab  program and participants went there when  they were usually at the end of their  rope they didn't have insurance they had  nowhere else to go many of them were  coming from state-funded detox  facilities or straight off the street  and they went into this rehab program  and then they were forced to work for  free in adult care homes as caregivers  for elderly and disabled people so you  had recovering drug addicts who had  almost no training who were changing  diapers for a really vulnerable  population and sometimes even dispensing  drugs that led to their addiction in the  first place so what we found obviously  in this the scenario things went wrong  all the time  found that these rehab participants for  stealing drugs from the assisted living  facility in several cases were accused  of sexually assaulting patients at these  adult care homes and this this just  reminded me of what you were saying was  that a lot of people that we talk to you  describe this rehab program more than  some others as very cult-like the only  sort of therapy that they would get was  every week they would be put in a circle  and someone would be in the hot seat and  then everyone else in the rehab program  would yell at them about their  flaws it would get incredibly abusive  there was a lot of profanity and people  we talked to said this was all about  control sort of breaking them down and  then turning them into sort of puppets  who would go to work and then turn over  their paychecks and they they said that  you know it was a lot of it was very  brainwashing and and that they were open  to that because these were people who  had nowhere else to go and they were  sort of in this incredibly vulnerable  time in their lives where they were  looking to get help and you  unfortunately have a lot of these people  who are preying on that and sort of  making money from from people who don't  have other options is there any reason  to suspect that the criminal justice  system and this could be like a specific  court in a particular place it could be  individuals within some of these systems  it is anybody even potentially getting a  financial kickback from some of the  rehab centers because you see a lot of  mandated and negotiated participation in  these programs in lieu of prison  sometimes for example what about the  possibility of kickbacks being sort of  in the system it's a good question and  we we haven't found that actually what  we have found was that many different  states particularly drug courts and  diversion programs have strict rules  that say that courts are only supposed  to send defendants to licensed rehab  programs for treatment so that's a role  on the books but what we found was a lot  of times the license to rehab programs  have incredibly long wait lists they  have you know nine to ten months until a  bed opens up and then court officials  just say okay well something is better  than nothing we don't want to send  people to prison so they turn to these  unlicensed programs even though that's  technically illegal and against the  spirit of many of these laws in  different states so we we found that it  was a little bit more a marriage of  convenience than any sort of corruption  a lot of judges thought that people they  sort of had this this sense that people  should take responsibility and  go to work and again pick themselves up  by their bootstraps and try to get  themselves clean and the overriding  thing that we found that in a lot of  course is that there's sort of you know  as as criminal justice reform is  sweeping the country everyone is looking  for alternatives to incarceration and a  lot of these work based rehab centers  are sort of riding that wave and  marketing themselves as a great  alternative to prison to the courts and  courts either because they they are not  aware of the rules or sort of have have  found that a lot of these license to  rehab programs have long wait lists have  sort of shrugged and said okay  somethings better than nothing and then  they're funneling people into these  programs that are often open to abuse so  how do these programs not get shut down  I mean how how are they skirting what we  would think of as licensure licensure  laws labor laws whatever it is that that  there could be a case made if a normal  employer so to speak was doing this how  are they getting around it all yeah we  found that a lot of these programs are  unlicensed and it comes down to sort of  a technical jargon a explanation if you  describe yourself as a Recovery Center  or a 12-step peer support Center in  North Carolina that means that you're  absolutely exempt from licensure and  what we found out is at least for the  program that I was describing earlier  recovery connections community state  regulators sort of helped them avoid  licensure and coach them to say look  there is this licensing exemption and if  you describe yourself in this way we  can't touch you and a lot of people who  have issues a lot of these rehab owners  that have us kind of sorted paths take  advantage of that so we found that in in  this program recovery connections  community the owner of this rehab  program in the past was an administrator  at a different rehab  program that was licensed but she got  into a lot of ethical problems she ended  up sleeping with one of her rehab  participants using people in her program  to perform personal favors for her  rather than having treatment she would  force them to sort of babysit her kids  and wash her clothes and clean her home  she was stripped of her therapy license  fired from that rehab center and then  she just sort almost overnight started  this different unlicensed program by  just describing herself as a Recovery  Center and nonprofit peer support center  so a lot of a lot of what we found it  comes down to sort of a technical  marketing of yourself and a lot of a lot  of states just just have very poor  enforcement of the rehab programs we  found that in Oklahoma for instance  there was a rehab program that was  sending defendants to work for free and  chicken processing plants that program  was unlicensed because it sort of had a  faith-based exemption and this idea that  these recovery centers were a great  alternative to prison and state  lawmakers didn't want to want to create  too many standards for these types of  programs to pop up they were afraid that  if they made the licensing process too  arduous if these programs would not  exist and people would be funneled into  prison so often it's sort of just hoping  that everything's going well and then  these programs market themselves in the  specific way and state regulators just  don't do anything until they are really  really serious abuses extraordinarily  disturbing how we've been speaking with  Amy Julia Harris is an investigative  reporter for reveal from the Center for  Investigative Reporting really great  work and I appreciate you telling our  audience about it thanks for having me  on

